WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
ai just turn classic short stories into playable 3D worlds you can walk through right now. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 200, Episode 200. Thanks for sticking with us. A writer named Ethan Mollick fed short stories by Jorge Luis Borges into an artificial intelligence system called Fable 5.1. Borges is a famous author whose tales twist around ideas like infinity, memory, and mirrors. Artificial intelligence, or ai here, means software that has learned

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.520
patterns from huge amounts of writing and pictures. Fable 5.1 did not just draw illustrations.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.840
It built interactive scenes that tried to hold the feeling of each story. Picture a school

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.720
play where the set swallows the whole stage, and you are allowed to walk across it. The

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:51.280
words become rooms, hallways, and objects, closer to a small video game level than a page

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.320
in a paperback. Malik had to give the system permission to quote the original Borgas lines,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.820
suggestions, so the scene stayed faithful to the text.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.960
Each finished story sits as its own little explorable area on a free public borges and 3D website.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.620
Anyone can click through on a phone or a laptop, and no account is required.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.540
Reading for class can feel flat, like staring at a map instead of standing on the street

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:15.140
it describes.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.700
A tale about infinity or mirrors can become a space you actually move through, which can

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.980
make the main point stick.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.340
students could present a book report as a walk-through instead of a slide deck.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.540
Artists, writers, and game curious beginners can borrow the same idea to turn a poem

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.300
or a short story into a quick visual prototype.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.000
Years of three-dimensional modeling skill are not the ticket in.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.200
The writing is, future classroom tools may make history lessons

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.020
and assign novels feel more like adventures you enter.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.160
Open that Borgas in 3D website during study time or downtime.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.780
pick a story world and notice whether walking it changes how the idea lands.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.960
The jump from paragraph to play still sounds like magic, so let me slow down and show

00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.800
the hidden steps.

00:02:00.777 --> 00:02:04.397
Telling a friend about a dream is the closest everyday version of this trick.

00:02:04.897 --> 00:02:09.537
You never draw the hallway, yet they start picturing doors, colors, and that uneasy feeling

00:02:09.537 --> 00:02:10.317
of being lost.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:16.097
An AI story world builder works from the same kind of clues, only it has to actually construct

00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:16.677
the hallway.

00:02:16.677 --> 00:02:21.697
First, it breaks the text into small chunks it can handle one at a time. The way you

00:02:21.697 --> 00:02:26.577
might highlight a chapter with two different colors. One color marks nouns that name physical

00:02:26.577 --> 00:02:32.477
things, like a maze, a library, or a mirror. The other color marks phrases about mood or

00:02:32.477 --> 00:02:36.277
movement, like being lost, looping forever, or doubling back.

00:02:36.977 --> 00:02:42.277
Next, it places those pieces inside a virtual room or an outdoor area, using simple rules

00:02:42.277 --> 00:02:46.997
about distance and size, almost like a ranging furniture from a written packing list.

00:02:47.597 --> 00:02:52.497
Amaze belongs in the middle, a mirror might face you as you enter, a long corridor gets

00:02:52.497 --> 00:02:53.497
the sense of walking.

00:02:54.117 --> 00:02:58.737
Light and shadow arrive last, the way lamps make a dark living room stop feeling empty.

00:02:59.337 --> 00:03:02.837
Pathways get added so you can move instead of staring at a still picture.

00:03:03.497 --> 00:03:08.217
The last pass compares the finished environment back to the original sentences, checking that

00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:11.377
the deeper point survived and the space did not just turn pretty.

00:03:11.377 --> 00:03:16.697
When those steps stack, the walkable version can surface an idea you skimmed past on the

00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:17.097
page.

00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:21.737
Same recipe applies whenever software takes a description and hands you something you

00:03:21.737 --> 00:03:22.437
can move through.

00:03:23.057 --> 00:03:27.337
Once you can picture that recipe, a lot of today's smaller tools start to make more sense

00:03:27.337 --> 00:03:27.697
too.

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:34.955
Open AI took the login wall off web search inside chatGPT, and digital trends reports the basic

00:03:34.955 --> 00:03:36.695
version now works without an account.

00:03:37.635 --> 00:03:42.515
ChatGPT is a chat app that answers an ordinary language, and its search mode pulls current

00:03:42.515 --> 00:03:47.035
information from across the internet, then explains it more simply than a dense textbook.

00:03:47.555 --> 00:03:51.735
It behaves like a search bar that also sits beside you and talks the results through.

00:03:52.275 --> 00:03:57.695
Open the ChatGPT website, type a real question, and watch a short summary appear with links

00:03:57.695 --> 00:03:58.635
you can check yourself.

00:03:59.295 --> 00:04:05.015
Try what are the main events in the French Revolution for a history paper, or best beginner strategies

00:04:05.015 --> 00:04:08.515
in Minecraft if you would rather see how game advice gets sourced.

00:04:08.935 --> 00:04:13.795
School computers and shared family laptops can run that test in seconds, which removes

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:16.975
the extra sign-up step that used to sit between you and the answer.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:23.039
The Verge reports that some websites have started blocking AI agents, which are helper programs

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:24.899
that browse pages for you automatically.

00:04:25.579 --> 00:04:29.819
When one of those helpers knocks, the site can detect the automated visit and refuse to

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:34.279
load the content, like a door staff member waving through people and stopping a machine

00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:39.399
that keep sending copies of itself. Companies want control over how their pages get used,

00:04:39.899 --> 00:04:44.839
and they worry that crowds of AI visitors could strain servers or copy material without permission.

00:04:45.459 --> 00:04:49.579
Trip planners and price comparers may soon hit walls on certain booking pages,

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:54.159
and hotel deals that used to appear could vanish behind an access denied message.

00:04:54.839 --> 00:04:59.739
The trend is early, yet it already limits what research and shopping helpers can grab from the open

00:04:59.739 --> 00:05:05.499
web. If you use AI to gather facts quickly, expect more of those locked doors in the coming months.

00:05:06.519 --> 00:05:11.539
Open AI is also chasing humanoid robots, meaning machines shape like people so they fit through

00:05:11.539 --> 00:05:16.779
the same doors and beside the same furniture we already have. In gadget reports that building a

00:05:16.779 --> 00:05:22.159
body to match everyday spaces looks doable. Teaching the machine to think and react like a person

00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:28.359
is the steep climb. Engineers already know how to design arms and legs at human scale. The missing

00:05:28.359 --> 00:05:32.139
Building Peace is a decision-making system that can handle surprises without a person

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:33.319
whispering every move.

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:39.359
Sam Altman, the company's chief executive officer, or CEO, has said the physical shape

00:05:39.359 --> 00:05:40.719
is only the starting point.

00:05:41.239 --> 00:05:45.939
Real progress in his view arrives when the robot understands spoken commands and can pick

00:05:45.939 --> 00:05:47.779
the right tool for a job on its own.

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:52.159
That is the difference between a costume that fits through a doorway and a helper that

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:54.179
can actually work in a kitchen or a warehouse.

00:05:54.179 --> 00:05:59.399
A much smaller making story spread on X this week, and it is almost the opposite

00:05:59.399 --> 00:06:04.762
of a corporate robot lab. After neighbors spent weeks hunting the same rodent, one person

00:06:04.762 --> 00:06:09.442
caught the rat and turned the wind into a golden three-dimensional printed trophy. They

00:06:09.442 --> 00:06:14.082
printed a small statue, covered it in three layers of gold leaf, and added weathering

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:18.342
so it looked like an old award, then gave it as a lighthearted gift to the neighbor who

00:06:18.342 --> 00:06:23.922
finally succeeded. A school or library printer plus basic craft supplies can turn an ordinary

00:06:23.922 --> 00:06:28.762
inventory event into an object you can hold, which is a weekend scale project, not a factory.

00:06:29.322 --> 00:06:34.522
Story worlds, unlocked search, locked websites, and homemade trophies all landed in one cycle,

00:06:35.062 --> 00:06:37.242
